HiveMind — Beta End User License Agreement (EULA). The Spanish text above governs; this is a
translation.

1. You may install HiveMind on your devices and use it, including for work, under a free,
   personal, non-exclusive, non-transferable, revocable license for the duration of the beta,
   for copies downloaded from https://hivemindai.cl or
   https://github.com/CristoSolar/hivemind-releases.
2. Unless we authorize it in writing, you may not copy or redistribute it (sharing the official
   download link is fine), sell, rent or sublicense it, modify, decompile or reverse engineer it
   (except where the law allows it despite this license), use its code or design to build a
   derivative or competing product, or remove these notices.
3. It is a beta: it may have bugs, change or stop working, and some features may become paid.
   We may end the beta with at least 30 days' notice on the site or in the app; your agents,
   chats and settings stay on your device.
4. HiveMind runs on your device and does not send us your conversations or files; it only asks
   GitHub whether a new version exists. What you send to your AI provider is governed by its
   terms. You are responsible for your accounts and API keys and for complying with those terms.
5. Agents act with your permissions and can read, write and delete files and run commands on
   your device. With «Editar sin preguntar» or «Autónomo» they do so without asking: use them at
   your own risk and keep backups.
6. HiveMind is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind. To the maximum extent permitted
   by law, we are not liable for damages, data loss, AI provider costs or other losses arising
   from its use.
7. Third-party components (such as Python, GTK, libadwaita, claude-agent-sdk and the Geist
   fonts) keep their own licenses.
8. This license ends if you breach it or when the beta ends; you must then stop using and
   uninstall HiveMind.
9. Governed by the laws of Chile.

Versions released before the proprietary notice (up to and including v0.1.0) were published
under the MIT License and remain available under those terms to whoever obtained them.
